card the tile with the least potential and that might be safe (not likely to give other players melds). Checking game history: opponents have played various tiles, but notably, previous player has Peng T6 and played T4, B3; next player played T6, B5; across player Chi B5 and played T7, T9. Tiles like B2, B3, T3, T8, T9 have been played or are around, so they might be safer discards.\n\nReason: I choose to discard T9 because it's a terminal tile (9), which doesn't fit well with All Simples goal (since terminals are excluded from All Simples), and it's isolated with only one copy in my hand. Also, unshown T9 count is 3, so there are still some out there, but discarding it reduces my terminal count. Keeping W tiles offers more flexibility for chows. T9 is also less connected to other tiles in my hand (only T8 is nearby, but I have only one T8). Discarding T9 now is a reasonable step to simplify my hand toward All Simples or other patterns.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
