2, B3** are consecutive and could form a chow with B1 or B4, though B4 is less available.  \n- **B6 pair** is valuable for a potential pung or as a pair for winning.  \n- **T1, T3, T8** are less useful due to scarcity of connecting tiles (T2 for T1-T3, T7/T9 for T8, but T7 is already punged by me, T9 scarce).  \n\nMost promising discard candidates: **T1, T3, or T8**, as they are isolated terminals with low connectivity. Among them, T1 has been discarded by next player (Play T1), so maybe safe. T8 was discarded by player across (Play T8), so also relatively safe. T3 has not been discarded much, but it's a terminal with limited use.  \n\nReason: Discarding T1 seems optimal because:  \n1. It's a terminal (Worthless for \"All Simples,\" but I'm not aiming for that yet).  \n2. It has been discarded by another player, indicating it might be safe from giving others pung/kong.  \n3. Keeping W5-W6-W7 sequence and B2-B3 potential chow maintains better tile efficiency for forming chows.  \n4. My hand currently has no clear pattern; I need to develop melds quickly. Discarding T1 reduces terminal clutter without breaking potential sequences.  \n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
